{
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "Quiz",
 "name": "Balance of Payments & Exchange Rates — Exam Questions & Model Answers",
 "about": {
  "@type": "Thing",
  "name": "Edexcel IAL Economics 4.3.3 Balance of Payments & Exchange Rates"
 },
 "educationalLevel": "International A Level",
 "hasPart": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Explain (4 marks)",
   "text": "Explain what is meant by a current account deficit and give one possible cause.",
   "answerCount": 1,
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "A current account deficit occurs when the value of imports of goods and services exceeds the value of exports , plus net primary and secondary income flows are negative overall. K It means the country is spending more on foreign goods and services than it earns from selling to other countries. K One possible cause is a strong (overvalued) exchange rate . When the pound is strong, UK exports become more expensive for foreign buyers (reducing export demand) while imports become cheaper for UK consumers (increasing import spending). A This combination of falling exports and rising imports widens the current account deficit. A"
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Examine (8 marks)",
   "text": "Examine how depreciation of the exchange rate might affect a country's current account balance.",
   "answerCount": 1,
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Depreciation is a fall in the value of a currency relative to others in a floating exchange rate system. K When the pound depreciates, UK exports become cheaper in foreign currency (more competitive) and imports become more expensive in sterling (less competitive). K In theory, this should improve the current account — export volume rises and import volume falls, improving net exports (X – M). An However, this outcome depends on the Marshall-Lerner condition : the current account improves only if the combined PED for exports and imports is greater than 1. K If demand for both is inelastic, depreciation may actually worsen the current account — import spending rises (same volume at higher prices) while export revenue barely increases. An Even when the Marshall-Lerner condition holds, there is a time lag explained by the J-curve effect . K In the short run, existing trade contracts are denominated in pre-depreciation prices, and consumers and firms take time to adjust their purchasing decisions. An After the UK pound fell approximately 15% following the 2016 Brexit referendum , the trade balance initially worsened before gradually improving as exporters gained competitiveness. A The J-curve shows the current account initially deteriorating (short-run inelastic demand) before improving as demand adjusts over 12–24 months. An"
   }
  }
 ]
}
